\begin_layout Enumerate
Iteratively eliminate all strictly dominated strategies.
 Which strategies survive?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
What are the rationalizable strategies?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{i}$
\end_inset

 is strictly dominated if 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ \sigma_{i}^{\prime}\in\Sigma_{i},\forall\ s_{-i}\in S_{i}:u_{i}(\sigma_{i}^{\prime},s_{-i})>u_{i}(\sigma_{i},s_{-i})$
\end_inset

.
 First, eliminate strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $4$
\end_inset

 since it is strictly dominated by strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

.
 Next, eliminate strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 since it is strictly dominated by strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

.
 Now, suppose player 1 decides to employ a mixed strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{1}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma=\sigma_{1}(2)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(1-\sigma)=\sigma_{1}(3)$
\end_inset

.
 Then, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u_{1}(\sigma_{1},L) & > & u_{1}(1,L)\\
3\sigma+(1-\sigma) & > & 2\\
\sigma & > & \frac{1}{2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u_{1}(\sigma_{1},M) & > & u_{1}(1,M)\\
2\sigma+5(1-\sigma) & > & 3\\
\sigma & < & \frac{2}{3}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

So, strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 can be eliminated since it is stricly dominated by 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, the remaining sets of strategies for each player are 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{1}=\{2,3\}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{2}=\{L,M\}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part b.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since this a two-player game, then by Theorem 2, then the set of rationalizable
 strategies coincides with the set of strategies that survive iterated strict
 dominance.
 Equivalently, we can write 
\begin_inset Formula $R=\Sigma^{\infty}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider a game between an employer and a worker, in which the employer
 provides the capital 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 and the worker proives the labor 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 to produce 
\begin_inset Formula $f(K,L)=\sqrt{KL}$
\end_inset

.
 The per-unit costs of capital and labor for the employer and the worker
 are 
\begin_inset Formula $r>\frac{1}{4}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c>\frac{1}{4}$
\end_inset

, respectively.
 The firm and the employee simultaneously choose 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

, respectively, and share the corresponding output equally.
 The worker cannot invest more than 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{L}>0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Write down the payoff functions for the firm 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{f}(K,L)$
\end_inset

 and the employee 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{e}(K,L)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Find all the rationalizable strategies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The payoff function for the firm is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U_{f}(K,L)=\frac{\sqrt{KL}}{2}-rK
\]

\end_inset

and the payoff function for the employee is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U_{e}(K,L)=\frac{\sqrt{KL}}{2}-cL
\]

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part b.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The best response functions for the firm and employee, respectively, are
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
K^{*}(L)=\frac{L}{16r^{2}}=AL
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L^{*}(K)=\frac{K}{16c^{2}}=BK
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $A=\frac{1}{16r^{2}}<1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B=\frac{1}{16c^{2}}<1$
\end_inset

 since 
\begin_inset Formula $r>\frac{1}{4}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c>\frac{1}{4}$
\end_inset

.
 We know that 
\begin_inset Formula $L\in[0,\bar{L}]$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{L}>0$
\end_inset

 is a constant.
 Accordingly, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
K\in\left[0,A\bar{L}\right]\\
\implies L\in\left[0,AB\bar{L}\right]\\
\implies K\in\left[0,A^{2}B\bar{L}\right]\\
\implies L\in\left[0,A^{2}B^{2}\bar{L}\right]\\
\vdots
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

By induction, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ n\in\mathbb{N}:L^{*},K^{*}\in\left[0,A^{n}B^{n}\bar{L}\right]$
\end_inset

.
 Then, as 
\begin_inset Formula $n\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $K^{*}=L^{*}=0$
\end_inset

 since 
\begin_inset Formula $A,B\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, the set of rationalizable strategies is 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{f}=R_{e}=\left\{ 0\right\} $
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider a second-price sealed bid aunction game with two bidders 
\begin_inset Formula $I=\{1,2\}$
\end_inset

.
 The seller has one indivisible object.
 The valuation for each bidder 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{1}\leq v_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 These valuations are common knowledge.
 The bidders simultaneously submit bids 
\begin_inset Formula $s\in[0,\infty)$
\end_inset

.
 The bidder with the highest bid wins the object and pays the 
\emph on
second highest bid
\emph default
.
 If the two players submit the same bid, each one of them obtains the object
 with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Write down the payoff function 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(s_{i},s_{j})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Does any of the players have strictly dominated strategies? If so, what
 are they?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Describe the set of rationalizable strategies.
 Can you eliminate any strategies on the basis of them not being rationalizable?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\emph on
weakly 
\emph default
dominated strategy if there exists another strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(s_{i}^{\prime},s_{j})\geq u_{i}(s_{i},s_{j})$
\end_inset

.
 Does player 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 have weakly dominated strategies? If so, what are they?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Consider now an iterative elimination of weakly dominated strategies.
 Describe one strategy pair that survives iterated elimination of weakly
 dominated strategies? Is this pair unique? What is the winning probability
 and payoff of each player 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 using this solution concept? Are these unique?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The payoff function for agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,2$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{i}(s_{i},s_{j})=\begin{cases}
v_{i}-s_{j} & \mbox{if}\ s_{i}>s_{j}\\
\frac{1}{2}(v_{i}-s_{j}) & \mbox{if}\ s_{i}=s_{j}\\
0 & \mbox{if}\ s_{i}<s_{j}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part b.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Take 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}\in S_{i}\equiv[0,\infty)$
\end_inset

.
 We claim that 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 is not strictly dominated.
 Suppose for contradiction 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 is strictly dominated.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ s_{i}^{\prime}\in S_{i},\ \forall\ s_{j}\in S_{j}:u_{i}(s_{i}^{\prime},s_{j})>u_{i}(s_{i},s_{j})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Case
If 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}^{\prime}>s_{i}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(s_{i},s_{i}^{\prime}+1)=0=u_{i}(s_{i}^{\prime},s_{i}^{\prime}+1)$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{j}=s_{i}^{\prime}+1$
\end_inset

.
 This is a contradiction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Case
If 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}>s_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(s_{i},s_{i}^{\prime}-1)=v_{i}-s_{i}^{\prime}+1=u_{i}(s_{i}^{\prime},s_{i}^{\prime}-1)$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{j}=s_{i}^{\prime}-1$
\end_inset

.
 This is a contradiction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore, neither of the players have any strictly dominated strategies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part c.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since this a two-player game, then by Theorem 2, then the set of rationalizable
 strategies coincides with the set of strategies that survive iterated strict
 dominance.
 Since no strategy was eliminated by iterated strict dominance, then the
 entire set 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,\infty)$
\end_inset

 is rationalizable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part d.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We claim that for 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,2$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a weakly dominant strategy, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ s_{i}\in S_{i}\backslash\{v_{i}\},\forall\ s_{j}\in S_{j}:u_{i}(v_{i},s_{j})\geq u_{i}(s_{i},s_{j})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Case
\begin_inset Formula $s_{j}\geq v_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(v_{i},s_{j})=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $s_{j}\geq v_{i}>s_{i}\implies u_{i}(s_{i},s_{j})=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}\geq s_{j}\geq v_{i}\implies u_{i}(s_{i},s_{j})\leq0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Case
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}>s_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(v_{i},s_{j})=v_{i}-s_{j}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}>s_{j}>s_{i}\implies u_{i}(s_{i},s_{j})=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}>s_{i}=s_{j}\implies u_{i}(s_{i},s_{j})=\frac{1}{2}(v_{i}-s_{j})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}>s_{i}>s_{j}\implies u_{i}(s_{i},s_{j})=v_{i}-s_{j}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}>v_{i}>s_{j}\implies u_{i}(s_{i},s_{j})=v_{i}-s_{j}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
Thus, for 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,2$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a weakly dominant strategy.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ s_{i}\in S_{i}\backslash\{v_{i}\}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}$
\end_inset

 is weakly dominated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part e.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider now an iterative elimination of weakly dominated strategies.
 One strategy pair that survives iterated elimination of weakly dominated
 strategies is 
\begin_inset Formula $(v_{1},v_{2})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Case
\begin_inset Formula $v_{1}<v_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, player 2 wins the auction with probability 1.
 Player 2's payoff is 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{2}-v_{1}$
\end_inset

, while player 1 receives payoff zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Case
\begin_inset Formula $v_{1}=v_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, both players win the auction with probably 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

 and receive payoff zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, consider an alternate order of elimination.
 After iteratively eliminating all weakly dominated strategies for agent
 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 survives.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{j}>v_{i}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{j}\in\left\{ s_{j}\in S_{j}:s_{j}\geq v_{j}\right\} $
\end_inset

 survives.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{j}=v_{i}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{j}\in[0,\infty)$
\end_inset

 survives.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{j}<v_{i}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{j}\in[0,v_{i})$
\end_inset

 survives.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, different orders of elimination may yield different sets of surviving
 strategies, so 
\begin_inset Formula $(v_{1},v_{2})$
\end_inset

 is not unique.
\end_layout

