\begin_layout Problem
Player 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 chooses rows, player 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

 chooses columns, and player 
\begin_inset Formula $3$
\end_inset

 chooses matrices.
 Player 3's payoffs are given in the figure.
 Show that action 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 is not a best response to any mixed strategy of players 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

, but that 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 is not dominated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

First, we show that 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 is not dominated.
 Suppose for contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 is dominated.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ \sigma_{3}\in\Sigma_{3},\forall\ s_{-i}:u(\sigma_{3},s_{-i})>u(D,s_{-i})$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $a=\sigma_{3}(A)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $b=\sigma_{3}(B)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $c=\sigma_{3}(C)$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $d=\sigma_{3}(D)$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ s_{3}\in S_{3}\sigma_{3}(s_{3})\geq0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $a+b+c+d=1$
\end_inset

.
 Now, according to the supposition
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
u_{3}(\sigma_{3},U,L)=9a+6d>6=u_{3}(D,U,L)\\
u_{3}(\sigma_{3},D,R)=9c+6d>6=u_{3}(D,D,R)
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

Adding these two inequalities we obtain
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
9a+9c+12d & > & 12\\
9a+9c+12(1-a-b-c) & > & 12\\
-3(a+c+4b) & > & 0\\
a+c+4b & < & 0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

But, this contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ s_{3}\in S_{3}:\sigma_{3}(s_{i})\geq0$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 is not dominated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Next, we show that 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 is not a best response, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \sigma_{-i}\in\Sigma_{-i},\exists\ s_{i}\in S_{i}:u_{i}(D,\sigma_{-i})<u_{i}(s_{i},\sigma_{-i})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{1}(u)=u$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{2}(L)=l$
\end_inset

.
 Below is the expected utility of each pure strategy for player 3:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u_{3}(A,\sigma_{-3}) & = & 9ul\\
u_{3}(B,\sigma_{-3}) & = & 9(1-u)l+9u(1-l)=9l+9u-18ul\\
u_{3}(C,\sigma_{-3}) & = & 9(1-u)(1-l)=9+9ul-9l-9u\\
u_{3}(D,\sigma_{-3}) & = & 6ul+6(1-u)(1-l)=6+12ul-6l-6u
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Now, we consider the two cases for 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Case
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $u<\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

.
 We would like the expected utility of at least one of the strategies to
 be strictly greater than the expected utility of strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{3}(B,\sigma_{-3})>u_{3}(D,\sigma_{-3})$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
9l+9u-18ul & > & 6+12ul-6l-6u\\
15l-30ul & > & 6-15u\\
(5-10)l & > & 2-5u\\
l & > & \frac{2-5u}{5-10u}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

and if 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $u_{3}(C,\sigma_{-3})>u_{3}(D,\sigma_{-3})$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
9+9ul-9l-9u & > & 6+12ul-6l-6u\\
3l+3ul & < & 3-3u\\
(1+u)l & < & 1-u\\
l & < & \frac{1-u}{1+u}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

So, if 
\begin_inset Formula $u<\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

 and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{2-5u}{5-10u}<l<\frac{1-u}{1+u}
\]

\end_inset

then 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 is dominated.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ u<\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ l\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 is dominated by 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Case
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $u\geq\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

.
 We would like the expected utility of at least one of the strategies to
 be strictly greater than the expected utility of strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{3}(B,\sigma_{-3})>u_{3}(D,\sigma_{-3})$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
9l+9u-18ul & > & 6+12ul-6l-6u\\
30ul-15l & < & 5u-2\\
(10u-5)l & < & 5u-2\\
l & < & \frac{5u-2}{10u-5}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Now, we must check the case when 
\begin_inset Formula $l=1$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
10u-5 & < & 5u-2\\
l & < & \frac{3}{5}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

So, for 
\begin_inset Formula $l=1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ u\in[\frac{1}{2},\frac{3}{5})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 is dominated by 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 Also, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ u\geq\frac{3}{5}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ l\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 is dominated by 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{3}(A,\sigma_{-3})>u_{3}(D,\sigma_{-3})$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
9ul & > & 6+12ul-6l-6u\\
-3ul+6l & > & 6-6u\\
(2-u)l & > & 2-2u\\
l & > & \frac{2-2u}{2-u}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

So, if 
\begin_inset Formula $u\geq\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

 and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{2-2u}{2-u}<l<\frac{5u-2}{10u-5}
\]

\end_inset

then 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 is dominated.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ u\geq\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ l\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 is dominated by 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $D$
\end_inset

 is not a best response, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \sigma_{-i}\in\Sigma_{-i},\exists\ s_{i}\in S_{i}:u_{i}(D,\sigma_{-i})<u_{i}(s_{i},\sigma_{-i})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

